The key Ontario ESA requirements (as of early 2023) are that employers with 25+ employees on January 1 must maintain written policies on both disconnecting from work and electronic monitoring; copies must be given to every employee within 30 days of preparation or any change.  

Option C meets the employee threshold and has both policies, but its practice of updating monthly while only redistributing quarterly necessarily exceeds the 30-day distribution window for at least some changes. That single procedural failure makes C non-compliant. All other options either fall below the 25-employee threshold on the relevant January 1 date (so no policy obligation exists) or satisfy both the existence and timely-distribution rules.  

Thus C is the only employer clearly out of compliance on January 2, 2023.}

11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The key Ontario ESA requirements (as amended by the Working for Workers Acts) are that an employer must have both a written disconnecting-from-work policy and a written electronic-monitoring policy only if it employs 25 or more employees on 1 January of the calendar year in question; once the obligation is triggered, each policy (and any update) must be distributed to every employee within 30 days.  

- A, B, D and E all have fewer than 25 employees on 1 Jan 2023, so none of the statutory obligations apply.  
- C has ~1 000 employees on 1 Jan 2023, therefore both policies are mandatory and every monthly update must be given to staff within 30 days. Distributing updates only quarterly necessarily misses that deadline for at least two months out of every three, placing C out of compliance on 2 Jan 2023.  

Thus C is the only employer that violates the applicable rules.}

