7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {D seems the clearest case of non-compliance. Employer D crossed the 25-employee threshold on 1 January 2022 with a headcount of 30, which triggered both the disconnecting-from-work policy obligation (due 2 June 2022) and the separate electronic-monitoring policy obligation (due 11 October 2022). The scenario states it maintained and distributed the first policy but never developed the second; that omission alone leaves it in breach on 2 January 2023 regardless of later headcount reductions.  

By contrast, the description of Employer C is more ambiguous on the distribution point. It says the employer “updates the policies every month and provides a copy of the updated policies to each employee every fiscal quarter” while also stating that the policies are “appropriately distributed.” If the quarterly distribution is treated as satisfying the “appropriately distributed” clause in the problem statement itself, then C may still meet the statutory standard; the monthly updates could be viewed as internal revisions whose formal rollout occurs on the quarterly schedule. That leaves room for debate, whereas D’s complete absence of any electronic-monitoring policy is an unambiguous statutory gap.  

The remaining options either stay below the 25-employee bright-line test on every relevant 1 January date or already satisfy both policy existence and distribution requirements, so they do not violate the rules.}

12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {D}

Colleagues 2, 4 and 9 have already laid out the decisive point: Employer D crossed the 25-employee threshold on 1 January 2022 (30 employees) and therefore became subject to both the disconnecting-from-work policy (due 2 June 2022) and the electronic-monitoring policy (due 11 October 2022). The scenario states explicitly that D “has not developed a policy on electronic monitoring,” so the statutory deadline was missed and the breach continues on 2 January 2023.

The head-count volatility mentioned in D (six terminations on 15 March 2022) is irrelevant; the ESA uses a bright-line January 1 snapshot for the obligation, and once triggered the policy duty persists until the employer falls below 25 employees on a subsequent 1 January. D never did so.

By contrast, the objections raised against C overlook two facts. First, the statute requires only that each policy (and any revision) be given to employees within 30 days; quarterly distribution of monthly updates can still satisfy that window if the employer times the releases correctly (e.g., distributing the January-through-March batch by 30 March). Second, C is described as having “appropriately distributed” both policies, which on its face indicates compliance with the 30-day rule. Unless we are prepared to read “appropriately” as a coded admission of non-compliance—an implausible interpretation—C meets every statutory requirement.

A, B and E remain below the 25-employee threshold on every relevant 1 January date and therefore incur no policy obligations at all. The only employer that both triggered the obligation and then failed to meet it is D.}

